---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { scenes } from '../../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { planets } from '../../../data/planets';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'The Planets', href: '/explore/planets/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Planets: functions and drives in a birth chart"
  description="In astrology, planets describe functions and drives: identity, needs, thought, relationship, action, growth, limits, and more. Learn them in context, not as isolated labels."
  path="/explore/planets/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>The planets</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In astrology, planets describe functions or drives: ways a person thinks, feels, acts,
        relates, grows, limits, transforms, and makes meaning. Everyone has every planet. What
        changes is how each one is placed, conditioned, and connected to the rest of the chart.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.planets.src}
        srcset={scenes.planets.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.planets.alt}
        width={scenes.planets.width}
        height={scenes.planets.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-8);">
      <h2>Start with the function, then add context</h2>
      <p>
        A planet is not a personality verdict. It is one part of a much larger system. Mars may
        describe how someone asserts, pursues, or responds to conflict, but Mars in Taurus is not
        the same as Mars in Gemini; Mars in the 2nd house is not the same as Mars in the 10th; and
        either one may be altered substantially by its aspects and condition. This is why isolated
        placement descriptions are useful beginnings, not finished interpretations.
      </p>
      <p>
        As you read, keep asking three questions: <strong>What function does this planet describe?</strong>
        <strong>How is it expressing?</strong> And <strong>what else in the chart is it connected to?</strong>
        That last question is where synthesis begins.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="container grid grid--2">
      {
        planets.map((p) => (
          <article class="card">
            <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">{p.glyph}</span>
            <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-xl); margin: 0;">
              <a href={`/explore/planets/${p.slug}/`}>{p.name}</a>
            </h2>
            <p class="card__meta">Through the zodiac: {p.cycle}</p>
            <p style="margin: 0;">{p.drive}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Nothing works alone</h2>
      <p>
        Planets are one vocabulary in the chart. <a href="/explore/signs/">Signs</a> describe style,
        <a href="/explore/houses/">houses</a> describe areas of experience, and
        <a href="/explore/aspects/">aspects</a> show relationships among chart factors. Some
        traditions also use <a href="/explore/dignities/">essential dignities</a> to evaluate a
        planet's condition. The useful question is rarely "What does my Venus mean?" in isolation.
        It is "What is Venus doing in this chart, and how does it belong to the larger pattern?"
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
